      "headline": "Theia Impact",
      "text": "A Mars-sized body, Theia, collided with the early Earth, leading to the formation of the Moon and contributing to Earth's tilt and rotation."
    },
    {
      "date": "-03800",
      "headline": "Late Heavy Bombardment",
      "text": "Around 3.8 billion years ago, Earth experienced a period of intense meteorite impacts, which may have played a role in shaping the planet's surface and atmosphere."
    },
    {
      "date": "-03700",
      "headline": "Origin of Life",
      "text": "Life is believed to have originated on Earth around 3.7 billion years ago, with the earliest evidence of life found in stromatolites and microfossils."
    },
    {
      "date": "-02500",
      "headline": "Great Oxidation Event",
      "text": "Approximately 2.5 billion years ago, photosynthetic organisms began producing oxygen, leading to a significant increase in atmospheric oxygen levels."
    },
    {
      "date": "-01800",
      "headline": "Formation of Rodinia",
      "text": "Around 1.8 billion years ago, the supercontinent Rodinia formed, bringing together most of Earth's landmasses."
    },
    {
      "date": "-0750",
      "headline": "Breakup of Rodinia",
      "text": "Rodinia began to break apart around 750 million years ago, leading to the formation of new ocean basins and continental arrangements."
    },
    {
      "date": "-0540",
      "headline": "Cambrian Explosion",
      "text": "Around 540 million years ago, a rapid diversification of life forms occurred, leading to the emergence of most major animal phyla."
    },
    {
      "date": "-0250",
      "headline": "Formation of Pangaea",
      "text": "Approximately 250 million years ago, the supercontinent Pangaea formed, uniting all of Earth's landmasses into a single large continent."
    },
    {
      "date": "-0200",
      "headline": "Breakup of Pangaea",
      "text": "Pangaea began to break apart around 200 million years ago, leading to the formation of the Atlantic Ocean and the separation of continents."
    },
    {
      "date": "-0066",
      "headline": "Cretaceous-Paleogene Extinction Event",
      "text": "Around 66 million years ago, a mass extinction event occurred, likely caused by an asteroid impact, leading to the extinction of the dinosaurs and many other species."
    },
    {
      "date": "-002.6",
      "headline": "Quaternary Glaciation",
      "text": "The current ice age began around 2.6 million years ago, characterized by cycles of glacial and interglacial periods."
    },
    {
      "date": "0000",
      "headline": "Holocene Epoch",
      "text": "The Holocene epoch began approximately 11,700 years ago, marking the end of the last glacial period and the beginning of the current geological epoch."
    }
  ]
}

