      "text": "Pope Gregory XIII introduced the Gregorian calendar, which refined the leap year system by excluding century years not divisible by 400 to better align the calendar year with the solar year."
    },
    {
      "date": "1752",
      "headline": "Adoption of Gregorian Calendar in Britain",
      "text": "Britain and its colonies adopted the Gregorian calendar, correcting the calendar drift by skipping 11 days and implementing the new leap year rules."
    },
    {
      "date": "1900",
      "headline": "Non-Leap Year Exception",
      "text": "The year 1900 was not a leap year under the Gregorian calendar rules, as it is a century year not divisible by 400."
    },
    {
      "date": "2000",
      "headline": "Leap Year Exception",
      "text": "The year 2000 was a leap year, as it is a century year divisible by 400, demonstrating the Gregorian calendar's rule for century years."
    }
  ]
}

